{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader nobleman and one of the most powerful princes of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. Born around 1140 he was the third son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His family held significant influence in the eastern Mediterranean due to their strategic location and military prowess. Raymond III inherited his title from his father Raymond II who ruled the County of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1153. Raymond III assumed control after his father’s death and became a key figure in the political and military landscape of Outremer.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Inheritance**\nRaymond III’s early years were marked by the turbulence of the Crusader states which were constantly under threat from Muslim powers particularly the Zengids and later the Ayyubids. His father Raymond II had been a staunch ally of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and had played a crucial role in the defense of the region against the Muslim invasions led by Nur ad-Din. After inheriting the countship Raymond III continued his father’s policies focusing on maintaining alliances with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader states. His position as a major landholder made him a critical player in the region’s politics and he often acted as a mediator between the various Christian factions.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was defined by his military campaigns and diplomatic efforts. He took part in several major conflicts including the Battle of Harim in 1177 where he fought against the forces of Saladin a prominent Muslim leader who was unified the Muslim world under the Ayyubid dynasty. Despite initial setbacks Raymond III remained a formidable opponent to Saladin and continued to resist his advances. One of his most notable achievements was his alliance with Bohemond III Prince of Antioch and Guy of Lusignan who was the husband of Baldwin IV’s sister Sibylla. This coalition was aimed at countering Saladin’s growing power and maintaining Christian control over the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Marriage and Family**\nRaymond III’s personal life was equally significant as he married Eschiva daughter of Joscelin III Count of Edessa. Their union produced several children including Raymond-Roupen who would inherit the county after his father’s death and Alice who married Hugh I King of Cyprus. Raymond-Roupen succeeded him as Count of Tripoli and continued his legacy of resistance against the Ayyubids. Raymond III’s family ties strengthened his position in the Crusader states and his descendants played important roles in the region’s history well into the 13th century.\n\nIn his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from Saladin who had consolidated power across much of the Levant. The Third Crusade in 1189–1192 brought new opportunities for the Crusader states as European forces including Richard the Lionheart King of England arrived to aid them. Raymond III played a supporting role in these efforts though his direct influence waned as the political landscape shifted with the arrival of new leaders. He died around 1189 or shortly after the beginning of the Third Crusade leaving behind a complex legacy as both a defender of the Crusader states and a shrewd political operator.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Legacy and Impact**\nRaymond III’s contributions to the Crusader states were substantial though often overshadowed by the more dramatic events of the Third Crusade. He represented the stability and resilience of the County of Tripoli during a period of extreme turmoil. His alliances military engagements and diplomatic efforts were crucial in maintaining the viability of the Christian presence in the eastern Mediterranean for several decades. Historians often view him as a pragmatic leader who balanced aggression with negotiation ensuring that Tripoli remained a key player in the region’s power dynamics. His death marked the end of an era for the county though his descendants including Raymond-Roupen continued his work in defending the region against the Ayyubids and other threats. Raymond III’s story reflects the broader challenges and achievements of the Crusader era highlighting the enduring struggle for control of the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035089056473225355}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01817992888391018}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4510277509689331}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09894715994596481}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -1.208800196647644}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.5093410015106201}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -7.390703103737906e-05}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -1.0894478559494019}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.09664856642484665}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.7742629051208496}, {"token": " one", "logprob": -1.1251873970031738}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.003360222326591611}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0007856381707824767}, {"token": " most", "logprob": -0.32201680541038513}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -1.9289554357528687}, {"token": " princes", "logprob": -1.5622806549072266}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.8283382654190063}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.02764357440173626}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -0.7769863605499268}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.400034933700226e-05}, {"token": " Jerusalem", "logprob": -0.0013709203340113163}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.3959313929080963}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.011559178121387959}, {"token": " late", "logprob": -0.7660239934921265}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.018413323909044266}, 